{# skeleton-variant: appliance-invariants #}
{# end skeleton-variant #}
{# skeleton-variant: product-binding #}
`tests/unit/plugins/modules/contract_subject.py` is product-adapted. It
registers actual public entry points, one of two explicit modes, and a fake
adapter. An existing-only subject requires a writer; an observational subject
forbids one. Both require an information entry point. Incomplete registrations
fail during collection. Observational-only products import no fabricated mutable
exports. Every public module must be registered. Each configuration registers
the same inventory and runs every applicable case with a fresh backend.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

{# skeleton-variant: transport-boundary #}
`Contract.granularity` defaults to `fieldwise`, which requires evidence of both
changed and unchanged declared fields. `atomic` tests an indivisible write with
failed readback, its complete stored effect and uncertain outcome. The fixture
runs both through the same executable, using only `limit` for the atomic case.
Either declaration keeps the complete effect, uncertainty and no-redispatch
checks. Synthetic lifecycle tests retain partial progress coverage.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

{# skeleton-variant: loopback-setup #}
`Contract.write_command` recognizes contiguous native argv tokens through
`WriteCommand.alternatives`: `update --` by default, `set --` in the second
configuration. Declare each native write form, such as `set` and `inherit`. The
start-failure case injects at `subprocess.Popen` after the real transport
prepares the command and requires one matching start attempt, unchanged storage
and zero dispatched writes. Fakes must cross that subprocess boundary.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

{# skeleton-variant: inventory-evidence #}
`Contract.literals` holds `LiteralInputs.accepted` module arguments, their exact
native `argv` elements and unsupported argument dictionaries in `refused`.
Defaults accept a shell-shaped label and refuse an overlong one. The second
configuration accepts `accepted` and refuses the shell-shaped string before any
command in normal and check mode. Accepted values must reach the executable
literally.

`test_transport.py` uses its own `transport_command.py` to prove raw argv and
secret stdin bytes, including a stream larger than a pipe buffer; empty stdin
refuses before dispatch. These shared executable cases need no product adapter
or options, so products need no extra free-text or secret option to meet
literal-input obligations.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

{# skeleton-variant: product-rules #}
The adapter implements `inject_host_identity()` at its prepared execution
boundary; the fixture substitutes only the numeric host UID. Products whose
locator derives identity internally use the same boundary. Public locator
arguments stay valid. The case requires `host_identity`, unchanged outcome and
storage, and no write.
Unsupported public locator shapes have separate mandatory product tests in
`test_cli.py`.

`Contract.environment_policy` defaults to `refuse`: ambient loader values must
fail with `invalid_environment` before any command. `neutralize` must remove or
empty them before `Popen` starts a child. The alternate fixture closes inherited
and module-level environment updates, then sets its explicit path and locale.
Both policies test every ambient loader key. Target records must retain that
locale and exclude secrets; neutralized empty values are accepted. Document the
effective policy and any separate target inheritance in the product locator.

`Contract.faults` uses `FaultInterpretation` with an independent `NativeFault`
for `exit_after_effect`, `exit124` and `reject`. The first two default to
`reconcile` without error or pending codes; rejection defaults to
`proven_refusal` with `refused` and no pending code. Without a pending code, an
observed complete effect requires a converged repeat.

The alternate configuration retains `validation_failed` after observed effects
and treats generic rejection as unknown. Its durable pending marker refuses
repeats with `unsettled_write` and no further write. Set `NativeFault.result` to
`retain_effect` for retained failure after an observed effect, or `uncertain`
for generic rejection. `error_code` names the stable native failure;
`pending_code` names refusal while the marker remains. Incompatible
result/effect declarations fail during collection.

Native faults dispatch at most once, preserve redaction and derive changed or
unknown outcomes from observed evidence. Only protocol-proven pre-effect refusal
reports unchanged after native rejection. Status 124 alone cannot prove expiry;
real deadline termination and transport nondispatch remain mandatory. A declared
pending repeat must fail unchanged with its stable code and preserve storage and
the single write count.

Export `CONFIGURATIONS` as a tuple of complete subject inventories: the
example's default `SUBJECTS` and its second executable configuration. Register
the configurations the product supports; each runs every applicable case.

Writer factories declare `expected` and `literal_expected` as
`ClassVar[Expectations | None]`, with concrete `Expectations`: `managed` holds
exact post-write values, `projection` the complete public result, `diff` the
before/after objects and `changed_fields` sorted public field names. The default
fixture declares flat values. Fieldwise writers also declare exact `partial`
managed state; atomic writers leave it unset. Malformed expectations and
granularity mismatches fail collection. Observational factories may leave both
expectations unset.

The second configuration accepts nested `settings` and publishes values with
their recorded origins. Expected storage includes the native origin map;
projection and diff retain nested `value` and `source` objects. Prediction,
normal writes, readback faults, timeouts and literals all use those
expectations. Storage comparisons preserve unrelated rows and undeclared values.
Input and public field shapes may differ; the adapter's `literal` input accepts
nested values.

A multi-step subject belongs to a product operation that actually has
several steps; independent fields in one command do not declare such an
operation.

`plugins/module_utils/_binding.py` and `_compat.py` retain their shared
structure with product policy. The client, hook resource, definitions,
documentation fragment, fake program, fake adapter, other unit tests,
integration targets and scenario tasks are product-owned. The fixture setup
target is product-owned because the executable, its seeded state and its faults
describe this product.

`foundata.utils` owns bounded byte transport, the monotonic deadline and safe
error translation. The client supplies the command grammar, parser, locator and
version probe. Credential input stays on stdin. Native concurrency guarantees
and locator qualification remain the product's responsibility.

The REST inventory extractor is omitted. Add a product-specific extractor when a
versioned CLI reference supplies a useful inventory; keep its output under
`tools/inventory/`, excluded from collection archives.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

## Troubleshooting

To recover a short-path directory left by a killed Nox invocation, find it as
`nox.*` below `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, or below `/var/tmp` without one;
`uv run noxfile.py` leaves two. Read its `owner.json` at the exact path. It
records the path, the creator's host, PID and creation time and the directory's
device and inode. On that host, the invocation ended if no process has the PID
or the process holding it started after the creation time. Also confirm that
none of your processes has the path in its environment or working directory,
because a session's children outlive Nox. Compare the path without following
links. It holds only `owner.json` and numbered links to temporary directories.
Delete those entries by name, keep the link targets, and remove the directory
with `rmdir`. Leave symlinked or replaced paths and uncertain creators for
investigation. Nox itself removes only the unchanged directory its invocation
created.

## Engine fields

`Field(kind="strmap")` describes a dictionary of string keys to string values;
empty strings are values. A write replaces the whole dictionary, and the
engine compares and diffs it as one field. `maximum_items` bounds declared
entries. `pattern` validates declared keys only, so an older stored key
outside that pattern stays readable. The existing `map` kind holds names
mapped to lists of rights.

`Field(kind="secret", readable=True)` compares a returned secret privately
on every declaration. `update_password` applies only to write-only secrets
and is absent from the argument spec when there are none. A readable secret
keeps `no_log=True` and is excluded from public state, including information
reads and predictions. Its changed name appears in `changed_fields`; a diff
uses the fixed marker `<redacted>` for that name, never a value or hash.
`empty_clears=True` permits an empty declaration to clear the readable secret.
Without that explicit policy an empty declaration is invalid. Both options
default to false, preserving write-only secret behavior.

## Releases<a id="releases"></a>

Each change users notice adds a fragment to `changelogs/fragments/`;
`00-fragment-template.txt` lists the sections. `release` turns the fragments
into `CHANGELOG.rst` and `changelogs/changelog.yaml`, so neither file is edited
by hand.

Releases require `releasing>=7.0.0` for the `release` command. Install the
development requirement in the maintainer's Python environment:

```sh
python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
```

`release build` runs the committed revision's Ansible sanity tests before
building the archive. Set `repository` in `.releasing.toml` before the first
release.

